    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p17-q1-6-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$\\sin^2\\theta-\\cos^2\\theta=-1$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "False. Using $\\cos^2\\theta=1-\\sin^2\\theta$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sin^2\\theta-\\cos^2\\theta&=\\sin^2\\theta-(1-\\sin^2\\theta)\\\\\\sin^2\\theta-\\cos^2\\theta&=2\\sin^2\\theta-1.\\end{align*}$$\nThis is not always $-1$; at $\\theta=90^\\circ$ it equals $1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q1-6-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$\\sec^2\\theta-\\tan^2\\theta=1$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True, where $\\cos\\theta\\ne0$. Using $\\sec^2\\theta=1+\\tan^2\\theta$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sec^2\\theta-\\tan^2\\theta&=(1+\\tan^2\\theta)-\\tan^2\\theta\\\\\\sec^2\\theta-\\tan^2\\theta&=1.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q1-6-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$(\\sin\\theta+\\cos\\theta)^2=1$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "False.\n$$\\begin{align*}(\\sin\\theta+\\cos\\theta)^2&=\\sin^2\\theta+2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta+\\cos^2\\theta\\\\(\\sin\\theta+\\cos\\theta)^2&=1+2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta.\\end{align*}$$\nThe cross term is not always zero.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q1-6-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$\\operatorname{cosec}^2\\theta-1=\\cot^2\\theta$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True, where $\\sin\\theta\\ne0$. Using $\\operatorname{cosec}^2\\theta=1+\\cot^2\\theta$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\operatorname{cosec}^2\\theta-1&=(1+\\cot^2\\theta)-1\\\\\\operatorname{cosec}^2\\theta-1&=\\cot^2\\theta.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q1-6-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$\\tan^2\\theta-1=-\\sec^2\\theta$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "False. The identity $\\tan^2\\theta+1=\\sec^2\\theta$ gives\n$$\\tan^2\\theta-1=\\sec^2\\theta-2,$$\nwhich is not always $-\\sec^2\\theta$. For example, at $\\theta=45^\\circ$, the proposed left-hand side is $0$ and the right-hand side is $-2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q1-6-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "$\\cot^2\\theta-\\operatorname{cosec}^2\\theta=1$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "False. Where $\\sin\\theta\\ne0$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\cot^2\\theta-\\operatorname{cosec}^2\\theta&=\\cot^2\\theta-(1+\\cot^2\\theta)\\\\\\cot^2\\theta-\\operatorname{cosec}^2\\theta&=-1.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      }
    ]
  },
